  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v5.2.3 - FIX: AudioPreprocessor.Resample had NO anti-aliasing filter. It was bare linear interpolation, carrying its own comment conceding \"for production quality, consider using a proper sinc resampler\". Linear interpolation does not band-limit, so decimating 48 kHz microphone audio to the 16 kHz speech models want folded everything from 8-24 kHz back on top of the speech, where sibilants and fricatives live. MEASURED on 5.2.2: a 10 kHz tone resampled 48k->16k survived at FULL amplitude (0.500 of a 0.500 source) as 6000 Hz, inside the band the mel spectrogram reads - so Whisper answered microphone audio with fluent, confident, entirely unrelated text. Any consumer resampling audio was affected. Now a windowed-sinc kernel (Blackman, 8 lobes) whose cutoff is the decimation ratio, precomputed per output phase - 48k->16k reduces to a single phase, so the trig leaves the hot path without changing an output value. No test caught it even though one exercised the method: AudioPreprocessor_Resample_Frequency resampled 44100->16000 but asserted only output LENGTH and that values sit in [-1.1, 1.1], and its 440 Hz input was far below the destination Nyquist, so it ran the real conversion and could not fail. ADDED: real microphone capture - MediaStreamCapture.OnAudioReady was DECLARED and never raised, and the only capture call was GetUserMedia(video: true, audio: false). StartMicrophoneAsync now delivers mono float32 via MediaStreamTrackProcessor at the device native rate, with OnAudioError/LastAudioError and a frame-buffer depth so a stalled WASM thread cannot silently drop audio; MediaInterop.FromAudioDataAsync converts a WebCodecs AudioData frame and THROWS on an unknown format rather than returning silence. /whisper now records, transcribes, and passes the decoder_with_past session. See Changelog.",
